### What is the problem distributed tracing solves?

In a monolith, a single stack trace tells the whole story. In microservices, one user action fans out across many independently running services, each with its own logs. No single log file contains the full picture.

```text
Monolith: one stack trace
  TransferService.transfer()
    -> AccountRepository.debit()
    -> AccountRepository.credit()
    -> NotificationService.send()
  Full story in one place.

Microservices: scattered across services
  Gateway log: "transfer initiated"
  Payment Service log: "debit request received"
  Account Service log: "balance updated"
  Notification Service log: "SMS sent"
  How do you connect these? -> Correlation ID + Distributed Tracing
```

### What is a correlation ID?

A correlation ID is a unique value generated once at the API gateway. It is propagated to every service via an HTTP header, and every service includes it in every log line.

```java
package com.bank.tracing;

import jakarta.servlet.Filter;
import jakarta.servlet.FilterChain;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletResponse;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.UUID;

@Component
public class CorrelationIdFilter implements Filter {

    private static final String HEADER = "X-Correlation-Id";

    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws Exception {
        HttpServletRequest httpReq = (HttpServletRequest) req;
        String correlationId = httpReq.getHeader(HEADER);
        if (correlationId == null) {
            correlationId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        }
        MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);
        try {
            chain.doFilter(req, res);
        } finally {
            MDC.remove("correlationId");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected log output (all services carry the same ID):
Gateway:      correlationId=abc-123 Transfer initiated for SAV2001
Payment Svc:  correlationId=abc-123 Debit request received
Account Svc:  correlationId=abc-123 Balance updated for SAV2001
Notification: correlationId=abc-123 SMS sent to Rajesh
```

### What is distributed tracing with traces and spans?

A trace represents the whole end-to-end request (trace ID). Each unit of work is a span (span ID) with a parent span ID, forming a tree. Spans carry timestamps for latency analysis.

```java
package com.bank.tracing;

import io.micrometer.tracing.Tracer;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class TracedTransferService {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(TracedTransferService.class.getName());
    private final Tracer tracer;
    private final AccountClient accountClient;

    public TracedTransferService(Tracer tracer, AccountClient accountClient) {
        this.tracer = tracer;
        this.accountClient = accountClient;
    }

    public void transfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Micrometer Tracing auto-creates spans and propagates context
        var span = tracer.nextSpan().name("transfer-saga").start();
        try (var scope = tracer.withSpan(span)) {
            LOG.info("traceId=" + span.context().traceId() + " Transfer started");
            accountClient.debit(fromId, amount);   // child span auto-created
            accountClient.credit(toId, amount);    // child span auto-created
            LOG.info("traceId=" + span.context().traceId() + " Transfer completed");
        } finally {
            span.end();
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Zipkin waterfall for traceId=abc-123:
  gateway           0ms  ────────────────────────── 250ms
  payment-service   5ms  ─────────────────── 200ms
    transfer-saga   10ms ────────────── 190ms
      account-debit 15ms ──── 60ms
      account-credit 80ms ──── 50ms
  notification      210ms ── 240ms

The slow hop is immediately visible.
```

### How is context propagated between services?

Context is propagated via the W3C traceparent header. Micrometer Tracing does this automatically for REST calls and Kafka messages.

```text
W3C Trace Context header:
traceparent: 00-abc123def456-span789-01
             │   │              │      │
             │   traceId        spanId sampling
             version

When Payment Service calls Account Service:
  HTTP header: traceparent: 00-abc123-paymentSpan-01
  Account Service reads it and creates a child span.
  Same traceId, new spanId, parent=paymentSpan.
```

### How does centralized logging work?

Each service writes structured JSON logs including the trace ID. A shipper forwards them to Elasticsearch, queried through Kibana.

```json
{
  "timestamp": "2024-01-15T10:30:00.000Z",
  "level": "INFO",
  "service": "payment-service",
  "traceId": "abc123",
  "spanId": "span456",
  "message": "Debit of 50000.00 from SAV2001 completed",
  "accountId": "SAV2001",
  "amount": 50000.00
}
```

```text
ELK Stack flow:
Service logs (JSON) -> Filebeat/Fluentd -> Elasticsearch -> Kibana
Search: traceId:abc123 -> all log lines from all services for that request.
```

### How do you propagate context across async boundaries?

When a service publishes to Kafka, it must put the trace context in message headers. Otherwise the trace breaks at the queue.

```java
package com.bank.tracing;

import io.micrometer.tracing.Tracer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.header.Headers;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TracedEventPublisher {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafka;
    private final Tracer tracer;

    public TracedEventPublisher(KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafka, Tracer tracer) {
        this.kafka = kafka;
        this.tracer = tracer;
    }

    public void publishTransferEvent(String paymentId, String eventJson) {
        ProducerRecord<String, String> record =
            new ProducerRecord<>("payment-events", paymentId, eventJson);

        // Inject trace context into Kafka message headers
        Headers headers = record.headers();
        var span = tracer.currentSpan();
        if (span != null) {
            headers.add("traceparent",
                span.context().traceId().getBytes());
        }
        kafka.send(record);
    }
}
```

```text
Without header propagation:
  Trace breaks at Kafka. Consumer starts a NEW trace.
  The two traces are unlinked.

With header propagation:
  Consumer reads traceparent from message header.
  Continues the SAME trace. Full end-to-end visibility.
```

### What is sampling and why is it needed?

In high-volume systems, tracing every request is too expensive. Sampling traces a fraction, often with tail-based sampling to keep traces that errored or were slow.

```yaml
# application.yml - sampling configuration
management:
  tracing:
    sampling:
      probability: 0.1  # Sample 10% of requests
```

```text
Head-based sampling: decide at the start (random 10%)
  Pro: simple. Con: might miss the one slow request.

Tail-based sampling: collect all spans, keep only interesting ones
  Pro: keeps errors and slow traces. Con: more infrastructure.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Logs, traces, and metrics map to the three pillars of observability. Traces show the path and timing. Logs show detailed events. Metrics show aggregate health. All are correlated by the same IDs. Standardize on W3C Trace Context and OpenTelemetry so every service speaks the same language.
